# child_repo_10_LOC (Level 2) - example environment file
#
# A tracked TEMPLATE, not a live configuration. It documents every environment
# variable the Level 2 /health server reads. There are exactly two, both below.
#
# Nothing parses this file at run time: no dotenv package is installed, because this
# composition carries zero third-party dependencies. `health.py` reads `os.environ`
# directly, so a variable takes effect only once something has exported it:
#
#     HEALTH_PORT=8100 HEALTH_HOST=127.0.0.1 python server.py
#
#     export HEALTH_PORT=8100
#     export HEALTH_HOST=127.0.0.1
#     python server.py
#     curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8100/health
#
# Do NOT source a dotenv file to load these (`. ./.env`, `set -a; . ./.env`).
# Sourcing executes the file as shell code, so a stray backtick or `$(...)` runs as a
# command. Read the values out of a local `.env` and set them explicitly instead, and
# never commit it. If a file must be applied without a shell,
# `docker run --env-file .env` is the safe form: that parser reads NAME=VALUE lines
# literally, with no substitution or expansion, so nothing in the file can execute.
#
# Resolution precedence for HEALTH_PORT and HEALTH_HOST, highest first:
#
#     environment variable  ->  config/health.json  ->  compiled-in literal
#
# That is THIS tier's chain. The three-link shape is uniform across the composition
# but the middle link's file is not: Level 1 also reads a `config/health.json`, while
# Level 3's runtime ships no JSON parser and reads `application.properties` from the
# classpath instead -- which is also why Level 3 has no `.env.example` and documents
# its two overrides in that properties file and its own README. The literal fallback
# is required rather than optional: it keeps /health answering when
# `config/health.json` is missing from a container image, which is precisely the
# failure a health endpoint has to survive, and `health.py`'s literals are the same
# 8000 and 0.0.0.0 shown below.
#
# The chain degrades one link at a time, so "set but unusable" behaves like "unset":
# a usable port is a whole number from 1 to 65535, so `HEALTH_PORT=`,
# `HEALTH_PORT=not-a-number`, `-1`, `65536`, `8.000` and -- deliberately -- `0` all
# fall through to `config/health.json` and then to the literal. The process starts
# either way. `HEALTH_PORT=0` is refused rather than honoured because a kernel-chosen
# port cannot be reached by anything configured in advance; all three tiers refuse a
# configured zero identically, and the same rule governs the `port` value inside
# `config/health.json`. (Port 0 remains legal as a bind-time *argument*,
# `server.create_server(port=0)`, which is how the test suite binds without
# colliding with a real server.)
#
# That chain governs these two listener values and nothing else:
#
#   * `name` and `version` are read once at import from `pyproject.toml`, with no
#     environment step: identity is declared by the repository, not injected by
#     whoever starts the process, so do not add APP_NAME or APP_VERSION.
#   * The resource path `/health` and the `status` value `UP` are frozen contract
#     constants compiled into `health.py`. `config/health.json` restates both so an
#     operator sees the whole served shape in one place, but a differing value there
#     is rejected -- the endpoint keeps serving `/health` and `UP`, and `server.py`
#     prints one warning naming the rejected value. There is no HEALTH_PATH and no
#     HEALTH_STATUS variable, and neither may be added: a settable path would move the
#     endpoint away from where a probe looks for it, and a settable status would let a
#     deployment misreport its own health.
#   * This tier prefixes both variable names. The apex application uses the bare
#     `HOST` and `PORT` for its own listener and nothing here reads either, so setting
#     them in this tier has no effect. The asymmetry is deliberate.
#
# No further variable belongs in this file: this application has no data store, no
# sign-in, no TLS to terminate and no logging framework. Every value below is a plain,
# non-sensitive default, and because this file is committed it must stay that way.
#
# The normative definition of the endpoint and of these values is
# `docs/health-endpoint.md`, in the apex repository of this composition.

# TCP port the /health listener accepts connections on. Default: 8000, this tier's
# reserved port in the composition, so this application can run beside its siblings
# on one host without a collision.
HEALTH_PORT=8000

# Address the /health listener binds to; validated, then handed unchanged to the
# `ThreadingHTTPServer` constructor. Default: 0.0.0.0, meaning every local interface,
# so a published container port stays reachable from outside the host. Set it to
# 127.0.0.1 to confine the listener to loopback during development. An IPv6 literal
# works too - `::1` or `::` - and the address family is selected from the literal.
#
# 0.0.0.0 is a BIND address only and never a destination: a probe always addresses
# http://127.0.0.1:<HEALTH_PORT>/health, because a health probe asserts the state of
# *this* process.
HEALTH_HOST=0.0.0.0
